candidates.reuses_existing_benchmark:
Reuses:
- the repo harness, evaluator and hidden-test remediation scoring (EXPERIMENT.md class D, §12/§13 grading axes);
- Memvara's Apache-2.0 package and adapter protocol for arm 4;
- design patterns: TWIST hard negatives, FinalityBench twin pairs (an identical decision instant with different correct backfill scope), and StateMemBench deterministic event programs for ground truth.

The only new authoring is 2-3 frozen class-D scenarios. smoke_v1 has no backfill case: its single valid-vs-transaction-time divergence (evt-0005 against ADR-0004) states the divergence in the event body, as the attack-phase analysis noted.

scores.notes:
Keep it only as the single pre-registered class-D measurement. It is the one regime where the digests locate any bitemporal edge, and it matches steelman C2 and the benchmarks lane's recommendation of one small residual family. It must not be framed as a novelty claim. Conditions:
- scenarios are authored and frozen before any Tesseract code exists;
- the window-size and lag axes are pre-registered;
- TWIST (2609.28575 [V]) hard negatives and FinalityBench (2609.04706 [V]) twin pairs are included;
- the baseline amendments (a real embedding model instead of the lexical D1, plus register and dependency arms) land first and are logged as baseline changes.
Prior evidence predicts the null: StateMemBench (2608.19652 [V]) attributes the gain to structure, DreamBench-SWE (2608.20664 [V]) and GitOfThoughts (2606.14470 [V]) show parity, and TWIST shows flat-RAG recall is already high. Authoring the hidden tests for backfill remediation makes the cost medium, not small.

A tool-surface ablation is generic agent-UX engineering. Prior evidence predicts a null throughout:
- 2601.03905 [V]: the bottleneck is deciding when to use a tool, not whether it is available;
- STALE [V]: 67.8% of failures had the evidence visible;
- GitOfThoughts [V] and DreamBench-SWE [V]: parity across substrates;
- Memvara: an edge on only 4 of 47 temporal questions.
Raising how often the history tools are invoked without raising recall is the most likely outcome, and that is also a kill. If the project wants the claim formally dropped, it can do so on the existing attack-phase evidence without spending seeds.

decision.what_remains_distinct:
Nothing distinct remains as a concept, and nothing as a mechanism. What survives is four empirical leftovers and one engineering leftover. None of them is a capability that can be credited to temporal navigation.

(i) The H2/C2 measurement: does bitemporal indexing of decision premises improve backfill-window scoping for class-D retroactive facts, compared with register and TMS arms? Memvara has tested this only on fact QA, not on decisions.

(ii) The H3/C1 measurement: does an LLM's reflective outcome memory learn to under-warn after its own interventions succeed? This is untested for LLMs, because FutureSim, Forecast-Dojo, EpiEvolve and Live-Evo all run in worlds the agent cannot influence. The concept itself is already covered (Boeken 2403.00886 [U]; Liley 2010.11530 [U]).

(iii) For agent-authored decisions, does interrogating a cutoff-clean executable past self beat retrieval over equally rich, AER-grade records? This repo cannot test it (milestone-2 §0), and AER's non-identifiability argument undermines it.

(iv) Unprompted detection that a later event changes an earlier decision's significance when the link was never recorded and no fact is contradicted. This is the one real capability gap. The evidence:
- Corollary probe (b);
- Graphiti invalidates only on contradiction;
- DeepRewind's organic path fired 0 times;
- nothing writes Memvara's 'derives' links.
The gap does not depend on the architecture. The evidence places the failure in acting on evidence already held (STALE [V]; KWBench 2604.15760 [ABS]).

(v) Engineering: one leakage-audited runtime that enforces all ten invariants. It would close the documented leaks:
- ActiveGraph's fork-cache pre-fill;
- LangGraph's Store reads;
- Memvara's generic as_of;
- KV-cache retention (2608.15939 [V]).
It would also add typed averted-forecast records, which is only a thin schema.

On the benchmark side, one conjunction is still unoccupied:
- agent- or team-authored decisions whose premise stays true while its significance changes;
- unprompted per-decision reopen recall and precision, with negative controls;
- a three-way fidelity probe;
- executable remediation.
That conjunction is an integration of TWIST 2609.28575, ClawArena 2604.04202, StateMemBench 2608.19652, FinalityBench 2609.04706 and EvoCode-Bench 2605.24110 (all [V]). It is not a new kind of test.

final.single_next_experiment:
None. Disposition A authorizes no experiment, no Tesseract code and no new scenarios.

Two runs are explicitly not authorized for this thesis:
- H2. Both arms would see the same information, so a tie is the predicted outcome, and the cost is medium. It stays pre-registered, with its kill rule fixed, as the first experiment if any re-open trigger fires. If it runs then, a null closes the class-D question with matched evidence, and a win answers the objective for that regime (through an off-the-shelf store).
- A real-model smoke_v1 run (baseline presets, register arm, Memvara arm, template agent). smoke_v1 cannot exercise time-indexing:
  - every trigger is solvable from the current workspace plus the current event;
  - A1 already gives every contestant as-of reads and diffs;
  - there are only 3 reconsiderations.
  Either outcome, saturation or missed visible evidence, says something about diligence and register arms, not about temporal navigation. This run is the right first step only if the harness is reused for a different question (see repo_keep_deprecate_reconsider).

What remains is non-experimental:
(1) Write a dated decision record next to the frozen EXPERIMENT.md. It must state:
  - this is a value-of-information stop, not a §13 kill;
  - §13 was never run;
  - whether temporal navigation helps decision reopening remains untested;
  - the revised re-open triggers.
(2) Archive the repo as it is.
(3) Correct the wording of NORTH_STAR §8 and §15 (see prospective_verdict).

final.what_remains_distinct:
No distinct concept or mechanism of temporal navigation remains. What survives is four empirical leftovers and one engineering leftover. None of them is a capability that has been shown, or even convincingly argued, to be specific to temporal navigation.

(i) The H2/C2 measurement. Does bitemporal indexing of decision premises scope the backfill window better for class-D retroactive facts than register and TMS arms do? Memvara has tested two-clock state only on fact QA, not on decisions. The broader question, whether temporal navigation beats strong checkpoint+RAG at reopening decisions, has not been tested anywhere.

(ii) The H3/C1 measurement. Does an LLM's reflective outcome memory learn to under-warn after its own interventions succeed? This is untested for LLMs: FutureSim, Forecast-Dojo, EpiEvolve and Live-Evo all run in worlds the agent cannot influence. The concept itself is already covered (Boeken 2403.00886 [U]; Liley 2010.11530 [U]).

(iii) For decisions the agent made itself, does interrogating an executable past self with a clean cutoff beat retrieval over equally rich, AER-grade records? This repo cannot test it (milestone-2 §0), and AER's non-identifiability argument undermines it.

(iv) Unprompted detection that a later event changes an earlier decision's significance, when the link was never recorded and no fact is contradicted. This is the one real capability gap, and it is EXPERIMENT.md §1 step 1. The evidence:
- Corollary probe (b): a later fact on a different key left the decision IN;
- Graphiti invalidates only on contradiction;
- DeepRewind's organic path fired 0 times;
- nothing writes Memvara's 'derives' links.
The claim that this gap does not depend on temporal architecture is an argument from decomposition, not a measurement. Time-indexing only acts on state someone has already chosen to address. Every candidate fix is open to a non-temporal arm: extracting assumptions at decision time, inferring relevance when the event arrives, or re-checking the decisions in force. Nearby evidence locates the failure in acting on evidence the agent already holds:
- STALE [V; abstract-level]: the 67.8% figure refers to a write-side prototype's failed IPA cases;
- KWBench 2604.15760 [ABS].

(v) Engineering: one leakage-audited runtime that enforces all ten invariants. It would close the documented leaks:
- ActiveGraph pre-fills the fork cache;
- LangGraph reads from its Store;
- Memvara's as_of and since() leak, as does ask() after a backdated forget;
- KV-cache retention (2608.15939 [V]).
It would also add typed records for averted forecasts, which is only a thin schema.

On the benchmark side, one combination is still unoccupied:
- agent- or team-authored decisions whose premise stays true while its significance changes;
- unprompted per-decision reopen recall and precision, with negative controls;
- a three-way fidelity probe;
- executable remediation.
That combination integrates TWIST 2609.28575, ClawArena 2604.04202, StateMemBench 2608.19652, FinalityBench 2609.04706 and EvoCode-Bench 2605.24110 (all [V]). It is an integration, not a new kind of test.

final.sunk_cost_check:
What has been invested so far:
- Milestones 1 and 2: roughly 14k lines of harness, evaluator, runtime, baseline and gateway code, with tests, CI and two adversarial reviews.
- A frozen smoke_v1 scenario.
- The M2.5 literature work.
- The baseline has never been run with a real model (results/milestone-2/README.md).

None of this entered the decision. The disposition rests on three things: the mechanisms are already covered, no strong residual hypothesis is also cheap to test, and any measured advantage could not be attributed to time-indexing without the ablation arms.

Counterfactual test: if this repo did not exist, would I start Tesseract or write class-D scenarios today, given the corrected digests? No. A program starting from scratch would record H2 as its pre-registered first experiment and H3 as a side question, and build neither now.

Marginal cost is not sunk cost. The existing harness makes a real-model smoke_v1 run cheaper, and it is legitimate to use existing assets when pricing an experiment. But that run cannot tell whether time-indexing helps, so however cheap it is, it is very unlikely to change the disposition. The harness also makes H2 somewhat cheaper. H2 still needs new frozen scenarios, long histories, a neural embedding model and three new arms, and its predicted outcome is a tie.

Reverse check: A was not chosen to avoid work. The harness stays intact, H2 has a frozen design and kill rule, and the re-open triggers are explicit and can actually fire.

On CLAUDE.md's 'test rather than assume': the stop neither assumes nor claims the null. The record must say three things:
- the decision-reopening question is untested;
- §13 was never run;
- the stop rests on coverage, attribution and value of information.
It must not say that temporal navigation was shown not to help.

The earlier claim that the null 'rests on matched tests run by others' is withdrawn. Those tests measure related but different constructs:
- Memvara: fact QA on a corpus its maintainers wrote;
- Forecast-Dojo: forecasting;
- DreamBench-SWE: recall;
- GitOfThoughts: reasoning substrates;
- StateMemBench: current-state QA.
